<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Praskovia Kovalyova-Zhemchugova</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1768">1768</a>–<a href="/years/1803">1803</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Praskovia Zhemchugova, Zhemchugova, Praskovia Ivanovna Kovalyova-Zhemchugova</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Praskovia Kovalyova-Zhemchugova was a Russian serf actress and soprano opera singer, born into the family of a serf blacksmith owned by the Sheremetevs, probably in the province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yaroslavl">Yaroslavl</a>. Trained for the opera company of Count Pyotr Sheremetev and his son Nikolai, she debuted at the serf <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a> of Kuskovo in 1779 and from 1780 appeared under the stage name Zhemchugova, &quot;the Pearl&quot;. Her most important role was Eliane in Grétry's Les Mariages samnites, which she sang for 12 years and performed for Empress <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catherine-the-great">Catherine II</a> in 1787. Nikolai Sheremetev freed her from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serfdom">serfdom</a> in 1798 and married her in secret in 1801; she died in 1803, weeks after giving birth to their son Dmitry.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/praskovia-kovalyova-zhemchugova.jpg" alt="Praskovia Kovalyova-Zhemchugova" width="330" height="453" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Nikolay Argunov · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1803._Nikolai_Argunov_(1771-c1829)_-_Portrait_of_Praskovya_Ivanovna_Zhemchugova-Sheremeteva.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Great households kept many of them, and some were trained as musicians, painters, singers or actors: the Sheremetev counts ran a serf theatre whose star, the soprano <b>Praskovia Zhemchugova</b>, was a serf blacksmith’s daughter.</blockquote><a href="/societies/russian-empire">Social Hierarchy of the Russian Empire</a></li></ul></section>
